{% comment %}
  The product information accordions - four columns in the design, each with a
  heading of its own and a stack of accordion rows under it.

  Shopify section blocks are a flat list, so "rows inside a column" has to be
  expressed some other way. This section does it by position: a `group` block
  starts a column, and every `item` block after it belongs to that column until
  the next `group`. Dragging a row from one column to another in the theme
  editor is then exactly what it looks like - moving it above or below a group
  heading - and no block has to be told which column it lives in.

  (The first version of this section did tell every row its column number, and
  wrapped a group accordion round a row accordion with a script opening and
  closing the outer layer at the breakpoint. The column numbers were miserable
  to set up, so they are gone for good. The two levels are back because the
  design asks for them, but the outer one is a button and a div rather than a
  second <details>, so there is no accordion nested inside an accordion and no
  breakpoint logic: the columns behave the same way at every width.)

  Columns are open on load and stay open without JavaScript - the `is-open`
  class ships in the markup and only collapsing needs the script. So a failed
  or blocked product-page.js costs the shopper the ability to fold a column
  away, not the ability to read it.

  A row can take its text from a product metafield as well as typing it, by
  putting the key in `Metafield key`. The metafield wins on products that have
  one and the typed text stands in on products that do not, so one template can
  serve a catalogue where only some products document, say, their craft
  technique, without the rest of them showing an empty row or none at all.
  Rows with neither a metafield value nor typed text are skipped, a column with
  no surviving rows is skipped, and the section disappears when nothing
  survives at all.

  Three things make that worth using rather than just possible:

  - Any metafield type works, because the value goes through
    snippets/pdp-metafield.liquid rather than being printed here. A list comes
    out as a bulleted list, a file as an image or a video, a page reference as
    that page's content, a measurement as "42 in" rather than as the JSON of
    the object Shopify actually returns. Before, everything but rich text was
    escaped and printed, so a list metafield arrived as one run-on word.

  - `Metafield key` takes `namespace.key` as well as a bare key, and a bare key
    means the `custom` namespace. So an app's namespace, or a second namespace
    of the store's own, is reachable without a code change, and every key
    already typed into an existing template keeps working untouched.

  - A product can override the row's heading too, at `<key>_heading` in the
    same namespace. A row keyed `fabric` reads `custom.fabric_heading`. That is
    for the product whose "Fabric" row is really about its leather, or its
    brass - the row heading is part of the copy, and on that product it is
    wrong. The block's heading still has to be filled in: it is how the row is
    recognisable in the theme editor, and it is what every other product shows.
{% endcomment %}

{{ 'product-page.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
<script src="{{ 'product-page.js' | asset_url }}" defer="defer"></script>

{%- liquid
  comment
    The Specification row is built from several metafields rather than one key,
    so resolve them up front - the liveness pass and the render loop then read
    the same values instead of asking twice and risking disagreement.

    Three of them are fixed, because the fit, the model's height and the
    sizing-up advice are on every garment and the design names them. The rest
    are label-and-key pairs typed on the block, so "Length", "Thread count" or
    "Drop" can be added from the theme editor without touching this file. There
    is no difference in how they render.
  endcomment
  assign spec_block = section.blocks | where: 'type', 'spec' | first
  assign spec_fit = product.metafields.custom.fit.value | default: product.metafields.custom.fit
  assign spec_model = product.metafields.custom.model_details.value | default: product.metafields.custom.model_details
  assign spec_reco = product.metafields.custom.fit_recommendation.value | default: product.metafields.custom.fit_recommendation

  comment
    The extra pairs are flattened into two delimited strings rather than kept
    as metafields, because a spec row is one short line by contract - "Fit:
    Relaxed" - and a string is the only thing this section can carry from the
    resolution pass into the render loop through a Liquid `assign`.

    That is also why these do not go through snippets/pdp-metafield.liquid: it
    returns block-level markup, <p> and <ul>, and a spec row puts its value in
    a <span> beside the term, where a <p> is invalid nesting. The handful of
    types that are not already a plain string are flattened to one here.

    '~|~' matches the delimiter sections/pdp-why-love.liquid uses for the same
    job: no label or value is ever going to contain it, and unlike a comma it
    does not appear inside the values themselves.
  endcomment
  assign spec_extra_terms = ''
  assign spec_extra_values = ''
  assign spec_extra_count = 0
  for i in (1..3)
    assign label_key = 'extra_' | append: i | append: '_label'
    assign field_key = 'extra_' | append: i | append: '_key'
    assign term = spec_block.settings[label_key]
    assign raw_key = spec_block.settings[field_key] | strip

    if term == blank or raw_key == blank
      continue
    endif

    assign ns = 'custom'
    assign key = raw_key
    if raw_key contains '.'
      assign parts = raw_key | split: '.'
      assign ns = parts[0]
      assign key = parts[1]
    endif
    assign f = product.metafields[ns][key]

    assign value = ''
    if f.type == 'dimension' or f.type == 'weight' or f.type == 'volume'
      assign value = f.value.value | append: ' ' | append: f.value.unit
    elsif f.type == 'rating'
      assign value = f.value.rating | append: ' / ' | append: f.value.scale_max
    elsif f.type == 'boolean'
      if f.value
        assign value = 'Yes'
      else
        assign value = 'No'
      endif
    elsif f.type contains 'list.'
      assign value = f.value | join: ', '
    elsif f.type == 'money'
      assign value = f.value | money
    elsif f.type == 'date' or f.type == 'date_time'
      assign value = f.value | date: '%-d %B %Y'
    else
      assign value = f.value | default: f
    endif

    if value == blank
      continue
    endif

    assign spec_extra_terms = spec_extra_terms | append: term | append: '~|~'
    assign spec_extra_values = spec_extra_values | append: value | append: '~|~'
    assign spec_extra_count = spec_extra_count | plus: 1
  endfor
  assign spec_extra_term_list = spec_extra_terms | split: '~|~'
  assign spec_extra_value_list = spec_extra_values | split: '~|~'

  assign has_spec = false
  if spec_fit != blank or spec_model != blank or spec_reco != blank or spec_extra_count > 0
    assign has_spec = true
  endif

  comment
    Pass 1. One flag per block, in block order:
      'g' a group heading
      '1' an item with something to show
      '0' an item with nothing to show
    Deciding liveness once, here, is what makes the count that gates the
    section, the count that gates each column and the render loop agree by
    construction rather than by three copies of the same condition.
  endcomment
  assign flags = ''
  for block in section.blocks
    if block.type == 'group'
      assign flags = flags | append: 'g,'
    elsif block.type == 'spec'
      assign live = false
      if block.settings.heading != blank
        if has_spec or block.settings.content != blank
          assign live = true
        endif
      endif
      if live
        assign flags = flags | append: '1,'
      else
        assign flags = flags | append: '0,'
      endif
    elsif block.type == 'description'
      comment
        The full product description, which the buy box no longer carries - it
        shows custom.short_description there instead. A product with an empty
        description hides the row rather than opening onto nothing.
      endcomment
      assign live = false
      if block.settings.heading != blank and product.description != blank
        assign live = true
      endif
      if live
        assign flags = flags | append: '1,'
      else
        assign flags = flags | append: '0,'
      endif
    elsif block.type == 'contact'
      assign live = false
      if block.settings.heading != blank
        if block.settings.instagram_handle != blank or block.settings.whatsapp_display != blank or block.settings.text != blank
          assign live = true
        endif
      endif
      if live
        assign flags = flags | append: '1,'
      else
        assign flags = flags | append: '0,'
      endif
    else
      assign live = false
      if block.settings.heading != blank
        comment
          `namespace.key`, or a bare key meaning `custom`. Split on the dot
          rather than searching for a known namespace, so a namespace this
          theme has never heard of works the day an app creates it.

          Resolved here and again in the render loop rather than once into a
          variable, because Liquid has no way to carry a metafield object out
          of this loop - only strings survive an `assign` across iterations,
          and a string is exactly what must not be passed on: it is the type
          that decides how the value is rendered.
        endcomment
        assign raw_key = block.settings.metafield_key | strip
        assign ns = 'custom'
        assign key = raw_key
        if raw_key contains '.'
          assign parts = raw_key | split: '.'
          assign ns = parts[0]
          assign key = parts[1]
        endif
        assign field = ''
        if key != blank
          assign field = product.metafields[ns][key]
        endif
        comment
          Typed text is a fallback, not an alternative. A row pointed at a
          metafield used to ignore whatever was typed on it and hide itself
          wherever that metafield was empty, so the only way to give a row
          default copy was to give up per-product copy for it entirely.

          Now the metafield wins where a product has one and the typed text
          stands in where it does not, which is the same order
          sections/pdp-why-love.liquid already resolves its columns in. A row
          with neither is still skipped, so "hide what this product has nothing
          to say about" survives - it just means both sources empty rather than
          one.
        endcomment
        comment
          "Metafield only" turns the fallback off for rows the design asks to
          appear only when there is something particular to say - the craft
          technique row, which should be absent on a product with no
          hand-block printing, embroidery, quilting or smocking rather than
          carrying a generic paragraph. It is ignored when no key is set,
          since that would leave the row permanently unreachable.
        endcomment
        assign allow_fallback = true
        if key != blank and block.settings.metafield_only
          assign allow_fallback = false
        endif

        if field != blank
          assign live = true
        elsif allow_fallback and block.settings.content != blank
          assign live = true
        endif
      endif
      if live
        assign flags = flags | append: '1,'
      else
        assign flags = flags | append: '0,'
      endif
    endif
  endfor
  comment
    Comma-delimited rather than one character per block: `split: ''` splitting a
    string into characters is Ruby behaviour this template should not lean on.
    No flag is ever empty, so no interior entry can be dropped, and a trailing
    delimiter is discarded - the list stays the same length as section.blocks.
  endcomment
  assign flag_list = flags | split: ','

  comment
    Pass 2. Which columns survive. A column is identified by the block index of
    its group heading alone - the rows are "everything after it until the next
    group", which both this count and the render loop walk the same way.

    Index -1 stands for a leading column with no heading, used when a merchant
    has left item blocks above the first group. Those rows still render rather
    than silently disappearing.
  endcomment
  assign starts = ''
  if flag_list.size > 0 and flag_list[0] != 'g'
    assign starts = '-1,'
  endif
  for block in section.blocks
    unless block.type == 'group'
      continue
    endunless
    assign starts = starts | append: forloop.index0 | append: ','
  endfor
  assign start_list = starts | split: ','

  assign total = 0
  assign live_starts = ''
  for start in start_list
    assign gi = start | plus: 0
    assign found = 0
    for flag in flag_list
      if forloop.index0 <= gi
        continue
      endif
      if flag == 'g'
        break
      endif
      if flag == '1'
        assign found = found | plus: 1
      endif
    endfor
    if found > 0
      assign live_starts = live_starts | append: start | append: ','
      assign total = total | plus: found
    endif
  endfor
  assign live_start_list = live_starts | split: ','
-%}

{%- if total > 0 -%}
  <div
    class="pdp-section color-{{ section.settings.color_scheme }}"
    style="--pdp-pad-top: {{ section.settings.padding_top }}px; --pdp-pad-bottom: {{ section.settings.padding_bottom }}px;"
  >
    <div class="pdp-section__inner">
      {%- if section.settings.heading != blank -%}
        <p class="pdp-eyebrow pdp-info__label">{{ section.settings.heading | escape }}</p>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- comment -%}
        The column count is the number of columns that survived, not a setting.
        A template with four groups gets four columns; one whose fourth group is
        entirely metafield-driven and empty on this product gets three, at full
        width, rather than three columns and a gap.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div
        class="pdp-info__grid{% if live_start_list.size == 1 %} pdp-info__grid--single{% endif %}"
        style="--pdp-info-columns: {{ live_start_list.size }};"
      >
        {%- for start in live_start_list -%}
          {%- liquid
            assign gi = start | plus: 0
            assign group_heading = ''
            assign group_attributes = ''
            if gi >= 0
              assign group_block = section.blocks[gi]
              assign group_heading = group_block.settings.heading
              assign group_attributes = group_block.shopify_attributes
            endif
          -%}
          <pdp-info-group class="pdp-info__group is-open" {{ group_attributes }}>
            {%- if group_heading != blank -%}
              <h3 class="pdp-info__grouprow">
                <button
                  class="pdp-info__grouphead"
                  type="button"
                  aria-expanded="true"
                  data-pdp-group-toggle
                >
                  <span class="pdp-info__grouptitle">{{ group_heading | escape }}</span>
                  {%- render 'pdp-icon', icon: 'caret' -%}
                </button>
              </h3>
            {%- endif -%}

            <div class="pdp-info__rows">
              {%- assign opened = 0 -%}
              {%- for block in section.blocks -%}
                {%- comment -%}
                  Walk forward from this column's group heading and stop at the
                  next one. `flag_list` decides what shows, so the condition
                  lives in one place - see pass 1.
                {%- endcomment -%}
                {%- if forloop.index0 <= gi -%}
                  {%- continue -%}
                {%- endif -%}
                {%- if block.type == 'group' -%}
                  {%- break -%}
                {%- endif -%}
                {%- if flag_list[forloop.index0] != '1' -%}
                  {%- continue -%}
                {%- endif -%}

                {%- liquid
                  assign raw_key = block.settings.metafield_key | strip
                  assign ns = 'custom'
                  assign key = raw_key
                  if raw_key contains '.'
                    assign parts = raw_key | split: '.'
                    assign ns = parts[0]
                    assign key = parts[1]
                  endif

                  assign field = ''
                  if key != blank
                    assign field = product.metafields[ns][key]
                  endif

                  comment
                    The heading can be overridden per product at
                    `<key>_heading`, for the product whose "Fabric" row is
                    really about its brass. Resolved only when the row has a
                    key at all, so a row with no metafield behind it cannot
                    pick up an override by coincidence.
                  endcomment
                  assign row_heading = block.settings.heading
                  if key != blank
                    assign heading_key = key | append: '_heading'
                    assign heading_field = product.metafields[ns][heading_key]
                    assign heading_override = heading_field.value | default: heading_field
                    if heading_override != blank
                      assign row_heading = heading_override
                    endif
                  endif
                -%}
                <details
                  class="pdp-info__item"
                  {% if section.settings.open_first and opened == 0 %}open{% endif %}
                  {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
                >
                  <summary>
                    <span class="pdp-info__heading">{{ row_heading | escape }}</span>
                    {%- render 'pdp-icon', icon: 'caret' -%}
                  </summary>
                  <div class="pdp-info__content rte">
                    {%- if block.type == 'spec' -%}
                      {%- if has_spec -%}
                        <ul class="pdp-info__spec">
                          {%- if spec_fit != blank -%}
                            <li>
                              <span class="pdp-info__specterm">
                                {{- block.settings.fit_label | default: 'Fit' | escape -}}
                              </span>
                              <span>{{ spec_fit | escape | newline_to_br }}</span>
                            </li>
                          {%- endif -%}
                          {%- if spec_model != blank -%}
                            <li>
                              <span class="pdp-info__specterm">
                                {{- block.settings.model_label | default: 'Model' | escape -}}
                              </span>
                              <span>{{ spec_model | escape | newline_to_br }}</span>
                            </li>
                          {%- endif -%}
                          {%- if spec_reco != blank -%}
                            <li>
                              <span class="pdp-info__specterm">
                                {{- block.settings.recommendation_label | default: 'Fit tip' | escape -}}
                              </span>
                              <span>{{ spec_reco | escape | newline_to_br }}</span>
                            </li>
                          {%- endif -%}

                          {%- comment -%}
                            The pairs typed on the block, after the three the
                            design names. Read by index from the two lists
                            built in the resolution pass, which only ever grew
                            together - a pair missing either its label or its
                            value was dropped from both, so the two lists
                            cannot fall out of step.
                          {%- endcomment -%}
                          {%- for term in spec_extra_term_list -%}
                            <li>
                              <span class="pdp-info__specterm">{{ term | escape }}</span>
                              <span>{{ spec_extra_value_list[forloop.index0] | escape | newline_to_br }}</span>
                            </li>
                          {%- endfor -%}
                        </ul>
                      {%- else -%}
                        {{ block.settings.content }}
                      {%- endif -%}
                    {%- elsif block.type == 'description' -%}
                      {{ product.description }}
                    {%- elsif block.type == 'contact' -%}
                      {%- if block.settings.text != blank -%}
                        <p>{{ block.settings.text | escape }}</p>
                      {%- endif -%}
                      {%- comment -%}
                        Same two links as the buy box's size-help block, and the
                        same reason the wa.me number is a separate digits-only
                        setting: wa.me rejects spaces and plus signs, so the
                        number shown and the number linked cannot be one field.
                      {%- endcomment -%}
                      <ul class="pdp-info__contact">
                        {%- if block.settings.instagram_handle != blank -%}
                          <li>
                            <a
                              class="pdp-info__contactlink"
                              href="{{ block.settings.instagram_url | default: 'https://instagram.com/' }}"
                              target="_blank"
                              rel="noopener"
                            >
                              {%- render 'pdp-icon', icon: 'instagram' -%}
                              <span>{{ block.settings.instagram_handle | escape }}</span>
                            </a>
                          </li>
                        {%- endif -%}
                        {%- if block.settings.whatsapp_display != blank -%}
                          {%- assign wa = block.settings.whatsapp_number | remove: ' ' | remove: '+' | remove: '-' | strip -%}
                          <li>
                            {%- if wa != blank -%}
                              <a class="pdp-info__contactlink" href="https://wa.me/{{ wa }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
                                {%- render 'pdp-icon', icon: 'whatsapp' -%}
                                <span>{{ block.settings.whatsapp_display | escape }}</span>
                              </a>
                            {%- else -%}
                              <span class="pdp-info__contactlink">
                                {%- render 'pdp-icon', icon: 'whatsapp' -%}
                                <span>{{ block.settings.whatsapp_display | escape }}</span>
                              </span>
                            {%- endif -%}
                          </li>
                        {%- endif -%}
                      </ul>
                    {%- elsif field != blank -%}
                      {%- comment -%}
                        The metafield itself, not its value - the type is what
                        decides whether this row is a paragraph, a bulleted
                        list, an image, a video or a page, and the type is only
                        readable on the metafield.

                        This branch used to handle rich text and treat
                        everything else as a string, which meant a list
                        metafield arrived as one run-on word and a file
                        metafield as the name of its drop. That switch now
                        lives in one place, shared with the size chart and the
                        "why you'll love it" rows.
                      {%- endcomment -%}
                      {%- render 'pdp-metafield', field: field -%}
                    {%- else -%}
                      {{ block.settings.content }}
                    {%- endif -%}
                  </div>
                </details>
                {%- assign opened = opened | plus: 1 -%}
              {%- endfor -%}
            </div>
          </pdp-info-group>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </div>

    </div>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "PDP: Info columns",
  "tag": "section",
  "class": "pdp-section-wrapper",
  "max_blocks": 32,
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Add a Column heading block to start a column, then Accordion blocks under it. Every accordion belongs to the column heading above it, and the number of columns is however many you add — the design uses four."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Any accordion can be filled per product from a metafield instead of typed text — open it and look under 'Per-product text'. Rows with nothing to show on a product are hidden on that product, and a column left with no rows is hidden too."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "heading",
      "label": "Section label",
      "info": "The small label above the columns. Leave empty to hide it."
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "open_first",
      "label": "Open the first accordion in each column",
      "default": false
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "t:sections.all.colors.label"
    },
    {
      "type": "color_scheme",
      "id": "color_scheme",
      "label": "t:sections.all.colors.label",
      "default": "scheme-1"
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "t:sections.all.padding.section_padding_heading"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "padding_top",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 100,
      "step": 4,
      "unit": "px",
      "label": "t:sections.all.padding.padding_top",
      "default": 48
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "padding_bottom",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 100,
      "step": 4,
      "unit": "px",
      "label": "t:sections.all.padding.padding_bottom",
      "default": 48
    }
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "group",
      "name": "Column heading",
      "settings": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "heading",
          "label": "Heading",
          "default": "Product details",
          "info": "Shown uppercase. Every accordion below this block, until the next Column heading, sits in this column."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "name": "Accordion",
      "settings": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "heading",
          "label": "Heading",
          "default": "Fabric"
        },
        {
          "type": "richtext",
          "id": "content",
          "label": "Text"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "content": "Per-product text (optional)"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": "Any metafield type works. Text becomes a paragraph, a list becomes bullet points, a file becomes an image or a video, a page reference shows that page, a measurement shows its unit."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "metafield_key",
          "label": "Metafield key",
          "info": "A key in the `custom` namespace, e.g. `craft_technique` — or `namespace.key` for any other namespace. Products that have it show it instead of the text above; products that do not fall back to the text above. Leave both empty to hide this row."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": "A product can also rename this row, by filling `<key>_heading` in the same namespace — a row keyed `fabric` reads `custom.fabric_heading`. Every other product keeps the heading above."
        },
        {
          "type": "checkbox",
          "id": "metafield_only",
          "label": "Hide this row when the metafield is empty",
          "default": false,
          "info": "Turns the fallback text off, so the row appears only on products that have the metafield. Use it for rows that should be absent rather than generic — the craft technique, for instance."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "spec",
      "name": "Specification",
      "limit": 1,
      "settings": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": "Built from product metafields — custom.fit, custom.model_details and custom.fit_recommendation, plus any you add below. The row hides itself on products where all of them are empty, unless you type fallback text."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "heading",
          "label": "Heading",
          "default": "Specification"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "fit_label",
          "label": "Label for custom.fit",
          "default": "Fit"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "model_label",
          "label": "Label for custom.model_details",
          "default": "Model"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "recommendation_label",
          "label": "Label for custom.fit_recommendation",
          "default": "Fit tip"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
